WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.819
Common telemetry sources include operating systems, endpoints, applications, identity providers, network devices, cloud services, databases, infrastructure platforms, and security controls.

2
00:00:13.819 --> 00:00:21.791
Each source offers a different view of activity and has its own strengths, gaps, formats, and retention characteristics.

3
00:00:21.791 --> 00:00:30.182
Identity systems can show sign-ins, authentication failures, privilege changes, token activity, and account administration.

4
00:00:30.182 --> 00:00:38.204
Applications may record requests, errors, transactions, administrative actions, and access to sensitive functions.

5
00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:43.034
Network devices can describe communications between systems and locations.

6
00:00:43.034 --> 00:00:54.179
Security tools such as endpoint detection and response platforms, firewalls, email gateways, and cloud security services often enrich raw events with classifications or alerts.

7
00:00:54.179 --> 00:01:02.031
A SIEM can centralize and correlate these sources, but it can only analyze the data that is actually collected and understood.

8
00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:06.112
Collection planning begins with security questions and important assets.

9
00:01:06.112 --> 00:01:19.921
Teams identify which activity must be observable, select authoritative sources, document ownership and dependencies, and confirm that expected events arrive with enough context to support detection and investigation.

10
00:01:19.921 --> 00:01:23.633
Maintaining a telemetry catalog makes this work repeatable.

11
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:36.813
A useful catalog records source owners, covered assets, event types, important fields, collection paths, parsing status, retention, access restrictions, cost, and dependent detections.

12
00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:45.653
This documentation helps engineers assess the impact of changes and helps analysts find the best available evidence during an investigation.
